{"hash":"77c8d2c0b57cadae","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":98.039,"said":"You have reached Baba Baidyanath. Look down into the black stone basin: the Jyotirlinga rises only a few inches from it, small and dark, and its top is broken and uneven. Tradition says that is where Ravana pressed it with his thumb, when he could not lift it again. Here, at last, the kanwariyas pour the Ganga water they carried from Sultanganj without once setting it down, and here the Lord of Physicians is asked for healing. Fold your hands. The Dwadasha Jyotirlinga Stotram, traditionally attributed to Adi Shankaracharya, sings: ‘Purvottare prajvalikanidhane sada vasantam Girijasametam, surasuraradhitapadapadmam Shri Vaidyanatham tam aham namami.’ ‘In the north-east, in the place of the blazing pyre, he dwells for ever with Girija; gods and asuras alike worship his lotus feet: to that Shri Vaidyanath I bow.’ Vaidyanath is the fifth Jyotirlinga of the Stotram. A shorter hymn of the twelve says ‘Paralyam Vaidyanatham’, and so Parli Vaijnath in Maharashtra is also revered as this Jyotirlinga. Pilgrims may visit the twelve Jyotirlingas in any order their journey allows; by tradition, the yatra begins at Somnath, the first, and is held complete with darshan at Grishneshwar, the twelfth, near Ellora. Let the bells ring, and let the lamps be raised. Bol Bam! Om Namah Shivaya.\n\nDid you know? The Jyotirlinga is about five inches across and rises about four inches from its stone slab.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1291},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1307,"end":1399}],"words":[[0.098,0.204,0,3],[0.314,0.252,4,8],[0.579,0.373,9,16],[0.963,0.385,17,21],[1.36,0.661,22,32],[2.945,0.216,34,38],[3.173,0.325,39,43],[3.51,0.216,44,48],[3.738,0.084,49,52],[3.834,0.325,53,58],[4.171,0.397,59,64],[4.579,0.541,65,70],[5.385,0.108,72,75],[5.505,0.661,76,87],[6.178,0.625,88,93],[6.815,0.325,94,98],[7.151,0.048,99,100],[7.212,0.288,101,104],[7.512,0.361,105,111],[7.885,0.216,112